  "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{-6t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png)",
